Package: copyfair
Type: Package
Title: Ability-and-Item-Conditioned Answer-Copying Detection for Multiple-Choice Exams
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Hera", "Hastuti", role = "aut"),
    person("Tri Zahra", "Ningsih", email = "trizahra@uny.ac.id",
           role = c("aut", "cre"), comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-2544-9627")),
    person("Loso", "Judijanto", role = "aut"),
    person("Muhammad", "Syaf'i", role = "aut"),
    person("Sarah", "Samben", role = "aut"),
    person("Suci Kurnia", "Putri", role = "aut"))
Description: Detects answer-copying (response collusion) on multiple-choice
    examinations by extending differential-item-functioning (DIF) logic --
    the same ability-conditioned diagnostic framework used in 'condfair'
    (Ningsih, Aman, & Nasrulloh, 2026, <doi:10.1177/01466216261484166>) --
    to the domain of error-similarity indices. Classical copying indices
    (Frary, Tideman, & Watts, 1977; Holland, 1996; Wollack, 1997;
    van der Linden & Sotaridona, 2006) compare examinee pairs against a
    single global or ability-marginal baseline, which can either mask a
    localized collusion signal concentrated at one part of the ability
    continuum, or over-flag pairs whose shared incorrect responses simply
    reflect a population-wide attractive distractor. 'copyfair' models the
    expected error-similarity jointly as a function of examinee ability
    AND item-specific distractor popularity, provides a wild-bootstrap
    omnibus test of localized disparity, and returns
    Benjamini-Hochberg-corrected pairwise flags with an expected-match /
    variance decomposition analogous to classical S2/omega-type indices.
